> am a member of the local board.  I have asked our lawyer's committee to
> address the policies and practices of FSU with regard to message content.
> They will discuss the question at their next meeting.  I expect they will
> decide to act.  I also expect that FSU will quickly change its practices.
> In fact, the university committee that suggests policy in this area has
> recently adopted a proposed revision that would clearly specify that
> newsgroup charters are suggestive and recommends that users honor them.  It
> also clearly states that FSU students, professors, and administrators should
> oppose any attempts at message censorship on the network generally or Usenet
> specifically.  As a member of the Faculty Senate, I will urge my colleagues
> to adopt such language.  If they do not, I will gladly file in Federal Court.
> I already have found others willing to do the same.

(End of quoted material. Beginning of new material by Lizard.)
(Please note, for clarities sake, that gmitchel is at FSU, not UFL. I
mixed those up once, to my great embarassment.)

Well, that more-or-less speaks for itself, I suppose. Thoughts? WILL
the ACLU declare that suspending access for "spamming" is a violation
of First Amendment rights? What do the many ACLU supporters/members on
the net think of this possibility?

I suspect most of them will disagree. But a lot of the 'rank and file'
of the ACLU might not b technically literate enough to truly understand
the issues involved, and react as if a Universeity had suspended a
student for merely, say, posting pamphlets all around campus or
publically supporting a 'fringe' political movement.

Please note that gmitchel is aware the case is not one of censorship
for message content, but for message *placement* -- that is, the author
posted his message to nearly every newsgroup he could reach. His
phrasing of the issue in terms of CONTENT, not PLACEMENT, is indicative
of the likely strategy to be used if, as he threatens, he files in
federal court.

Do we really wish to see the Supreme Court rule that Internet access is
a right which cannot be denied regardless of how much an individual
abuses the net? That might just be the "imminent death of the net"
we've heard about for so long.

As a final note, let me state that I am not implying the ACLU has
already made such a decision, or is even likely to make such a
decision. I am trying to nip a *potential* threat in the bud, so to
speak, by calling this to the attention of the net community before it
spreads beyond a tiny local branch of the ACLU. The more momentum such
a concept builds, the harder it will be to stop it.

Spamming is NOT free speech, any more than shouting down a speaker you
do not like is free speech. It is an abuse of net access and a breach
of the spirit of cooperation and personal responsibility which makes
the Internet and Usenet possible.

