<greeny@top.cis.syr.edu> writes:
> gmitchel@garnet.acns.fsu.edu writes:
> 
> > > Vague rules at a state university, especially when applied to speech,
> > > are illegal rules.  A student at U. of Wisconsin was punished for
> > > sending racist email in violation of university speech codes. A
> > > federal judge said that the university acted illegally because the
> > > speech code was too vague and broad.
> > 
> > The reason is that rules must be specific enough that a reasonable person
> > would know precisely what behavior the rule proscribes.  Rules that allow
> > someone to impose arbitrary interpretations do not give satisfactory
> > warning that the behavior violates the rule.
> 
> That speaks to vagueness.
> 
> 
> > "Hate speech" are notorious examples.  If you criticize some group, such as
> > the Nation of Islam, others may interpret your criticism as "hate speech"
> > and attempt to have you expelled or punished for violating the student
> > codes.
> > 
> > At state universities, BTW, "hate speech" is vulnerable to legal challenge
> > not only for being vague and overly-broad but also as restriction of free
> > expression under the First Amendment and a violation of civil rights
> > statutes.
> 
> Overbroadness is specifically an issue with respect to the First Amendment. 
> It refers to a rule that is so broad that it proscribes a substantial amount
> of constitutionally-protected expression along with constitutionally-
> unprotected expression.  It is generally relevant only at public
> institutions.  (California is a notable exception.)
> 
> What civil rights statutes do you refer to as governing this area?  I'm aware
> of a relevant statute in California.  Are there some others of which we
> should be aware?
>
Yes.  U.S. statutory law prohibits interference with the exercise of civil
rights.  This applies not just to actions by the U.S. government but also
actions by individuals.

It has been used by the U.S. Justice Department to bring murderers of
Medgar Evers and other civil rights workers to justice before U.S. Federal
Courts.

It is also frequently used in police misconduct cases.

You should especially take a look at USC 1983.

 
>  
> > At private universities, many have provisions of free expression and
> > academic freedom.  "Hate speech" clauses in student handbooks may violate
> > those provisions.  One could then make a legal argument that the provisions
> > of expression and academic freedom, being central to the mission of a
> > university, supercede the provisions of a student code.  My *guess* is that
> > most courts would agree, since courts generally do not like interference
> > free expression.
> 
> Hmm.  The only generalization I'd probably make with respect to the courts
> and such issues is that they are probably likely to defer to the school
> administration.
> 
I suspect the opposite is the case, if you look at the actual cases.  What
university administrators count on is the inability of students to effectively
fight them.  Most students will succumb to pressure from administrators and
parents to keep quiet and walk away with their degrees.

My argument is that most private universities also proclaim academic freedom
and free expression as operative rules.  A few with strong religious
affiliations do not, but I think they are the exception rather than the rule.
Certainly Ivy League schools such as Harvard, Yale, etc. make this claim.
One could argue that such claims constitute a contract.  If students or
faculty rely upon those representations to their detriment (i.e., are
disciplined for expression of unpopular ideas), then the university has
breached it contractual obligations and is therefore liable under contract
law.

> In the context of a private school, it is basically a contract issue.  If you
> can prove that there was an implied contractual obligation for the school to
> protect expression, you win.  If not, you lose.  I wouldn't bet on winning if
> the published regulations include broad proscriptions on "hate speech." 
> (That is, winning on the grounds that a contractual provision protects
> expression.  You might still win on grounds of vagueness.)
> 
You are right.  I would modify this a bit and say that it depends on the
public statements made by the university.  If the university claims to
subscribe to academic freedom and free expression, its case for enforcing
political correctness diminishes.


> I'd almost certainly look to get a case of this sort into federal court, if
> possible.  In a lower state court, it's too likely that you run into a judge
> who is partial to the administration (perhaps even an alumnus).
> 
That is true.

I prefer Federal Court to state court for most cases involving civil
liberties.  On average, I think judges in Federal Court take a more
protective stance on individual liberties.

I believe it is important to keep in mind that winning a lawsuit is difficult.
This is true for both sides.  When universities win, the victory is often a
Pyrrhic victory.  They lose a lot of credibility.  Their worthiness of being
called a university (the name itself suggesting openness to all viewpoints)
comes into serious question.  Advocates of free speech on university campuses
can use this to their advantage.

In our case, the new policy that is proposed for computer access has just
come out of the Faculty Senate committee (with unanimous approval).  The
relevant section reads:

   "Material accessible to the FSU community through networks and
    material disseminated from FSU should not be restricted on the
    basis of its content, nor because of the origin, background, or
    views of those contributing to its creation (note: obscenity is
    not protected by the First Amendment).  University administrators,
    faculty, and staff should challenge any attempts to censor
    electronic information sources."

I did not attend this meeting, because I wanted to see what would
happen if the committee was left to its own devices.  I did have
two observers there.  The fact that intended to take the question
of Usenet message censorship to Federal Court (if necessary) was
mentioned openly and colored the discussion on this point.  To the
committee's credit, the discussion was free of controversy.  The
chair of the committee asserted that it was easy to pressure faculty
via their department chairs.  The driving force behind our local
Freenet (run from the university) asserted his right to censor and
his intent to continue.  Those issues still need to be addressed,
but on the whole the outcome was very positive for free expression.

If you stand your ground, and you are prepared to fight for free
expression, there is hope of victory.  It takes time and energy,
and the personal costs can be high, but progress can occur.


